'''Hachina''' is the language of the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]] people and the language of the state of [[Hachinawa]]. It is spoken on the central plain of [[Sinchew]] and, as the language of the imperial court, was used in the sixteenth century throughout the territory the empire governed, including the uplands of its tributaries.

It is the larger of the two languages of the Hachina family, the other being [[Hatako]].

== Classification ==

Hachina belongs to the Hachina family, which comprises two languages, Hachina and Hatako. The family is not related to any of the other language families of Sinchew, and the relationship between the two languages within it is close: they are held to have diverged within the region rather than to have arrived separately.

The classification of the family relative to the others is unsettled, and no relationship to the [[Nahola languages|Nahola]], [[Uwisa language|Uwisa]] or [[Yelani language|Yelani]] families has been demonstrated.

== Phonology ==

The consonant inventory comprises stops (p, t, k, b, d, g), affricates (ch, ts), fricatives (f, s, sh, h), nasals (m, n), approximants (w, y, l) and a glottal stop. The vowel inventory is five, a e i o u, occurring short and long, with nasalised forms in some environments.

Syllables are of the form CV and CVC, with consonant clusters rare and generally restricted to a stop followed by a glide. Words commonly end in a vowel, and two and three syllable words are the ordinary forms.

Hachina is not tonal. Pitch is not used to distinguish words, and a syllable has the same meaning whatever pitch it is given. Nasalisation and vowel length are the distinctions the language makes, and they are distinctions of quality and quantity rather than of pitch.

The absence of tone is the principal structural difference between Hachina and [[Zong language|Zong]], which is tonal. It is also the reason the writing of Hachina in Zong characters produced a simpler set of readings than Zong itself has, since a Hachina character carries one reading where a Zong character may carry several.

== Grammar ==

Hachina marks possession by a set of prefixes agreeing with the possessor, and distinguishes inclusive and exclusive forms in the first person plural. The verb carries suffixes marking aspect rather than tense.

Word order in the ordinary clause is verb-final. The order is inverted in formal and ritual speech, which is one of the marks distinguishing the registers of the court from those of the plain.

